<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Chat from './lib/Chat.abide'
import Conversation from './lib/Conversation.abide'
import LimitedChat from './lib/LimitedChat.abide'
import TrimmedChat from './lib/TrimmedChat.abide'
import chatSource from './lib/Chat.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import conversationSource from './lib/Conversation.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import lastSpokenSource from './lib/LastSpoken.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import limitedChatSource from './lib/LimitedChat.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import onboardingSource from './lib/onboarding.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import trimmedChatSource from './lib/TrimmedChat.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The forms of a channel">
        <p>A channel is not a factory of its own. It is a <code>reactive</code> over an array,
        <code>patch</code> is how a message is appended, and <code>by</code> is what tells one
        message from another.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'reactive<Turn[]>([], { by: \'id\' })'}</code></td><td>one channel — a <code>Reactive</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'reactive((_args: Args): Turn[] => [], { by: \'id\' })'}</code></td><td>a <code>Keyed</code>, one channel per args key</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'turns({ conversation })'}</code></td><td>the channel for that key — a <code>Reactive</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>room.patch((list) =&gt; list.push(m))</code> appends in place (5.7). The latest
        message is <code>room.at(-1)</code>, and <code>{'{#for m of room}'}</code> is the whole
        conversation. How long the array grows is yours to decide: abide puts no cap on it, and the
        trim goes in the same patch as the push.</p>

        <p>Every other option is the one any reactive value takes. <code>args</code> refuses a key
        (11.28), <code>transform</code> shapes what a <code>set</code> stores, and <code>ttl</code>
        is the life of a keyed channel's entry (11.45). On a channel a <code>GET</code> serves,
        <code>refresh</code> and <code>invalidate</code> reach every subscriber's copy (7.10, 7.18).
        Every signature is in the <a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>reactive</code>
        reference</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Anyone appends, anyone reads">
        <Example
            id="channel-shared"
            title="`reactive<Turn[]>([])` — a channel anything can reach"
            summary="A conversation's composer, and the line above it that reports who spoke last. Press Send and watch a component the composer never names."
            files={{
                'lib/onboarding.ts': onboardingSource,
                'lib/Chat.abide': chatSource,
                'lib/LastSpoken.abide': lastSpokenSource,
            }}
        >
            <Chat />
        </Example>

        <p><em>2 components, 0 props threaded</em> — against the subscriber list the hand-written
        arm keeps, and the wiring it takes per reader.</p>

        <p>That symmetry is the whole difference. A value you write belongs to whoever owns it; a
        derived value is computed by its body; a channel is written by <strong>anything that can
        reach it</strong>, and read the same way. The composer imports the channel and not the
        readout, the readout imports the channel and not the composer, and the channel is the whole
        of what is between them.</p>

        <p>What comes back is a <code>Reactive</code>, so there is no second type to learn either.
        <code>LastSpoken</code> derives <code>last</code> as <code>reactive(() =&gt;
        onboarding.at(-1))</code>, which follows the channel the way any derived value follows what
        it read. Opened with <code>[]</code>, a channel has landed: it is <code>ready()</code>
        before anyone speaks, and <code>last</code> reads <code>undefined</code> until somebody
        does.</p>

        <p>Nothing is loading where a channel is written, so it is never <code>busy()</code> there
        (3.9). A subscriber's copy is <code>busy()</code> until the first value arrives over the
        connection (9.14), so an <code>await</code> there resolves at that value (2.7).</p>

        <p>In-process nobody asks who is writing — the page appends because it is your code. A
        caller <strong>outside</strong> the process reaches a channel only through a handler you
        declare: a <code>GET</code> to follow it, and a <code>POST</code> whose handler patches it.
        So there is no way in from outside that you did not write.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel per args key">
        <Example
            id="channel-rooms"
            title="`reactive((args) => [])` — a channel per key"
            summary="Two conversations behind one declaration. Send something in onboarding, switch to billing, send there, and come back."
            files={{
                'lib/Conversation.abide': conversationSource,
            }}
        >
            <Conversation />
        </Example>

        <p>Give the body a parameter and it is <strong>one channel per key</strong> (11.2), and the
        args you call it with pick one. That one declaration is a different channel per
        conversation, which is why changing conversations is changing one value and nothing else.
        Each holds its own messages, and coming back to one finds it as you left it.</p>

        <p>The body answers <code>[]</code>, so a conversation nobody has written to lands empty —
        the empty conversation on the card. The parameter has to be declared, or the call is the
        unkeyed form.</p>

        <p>Args are keyed by the same canonical wire form every keyed value uses — sorted,
        <code>undefined</code> dropped, <code>Date</code> written ISO — so a fresh object built each
        run lands on the same channel (11.24). The <code>args</code> option is a schema that
        <strong>refuses</strong> a key rather than normalizing one, two spellings that both pass
        being two channels (11.28).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel's length is its writer's to decide">
        <Example
            id="channel-tail"
            title="`patch` with a trim — a channel that keeps three turns"
            summary="A transcript pane over a conversation already three turns old. Send a fourth and watch the top of it."
            files={{
                'lib/TrimmedChat.abide': trimmedChatSource,
            }}
        >
            <TrimmedChat />
        </Example>

        <p>The trim is one line in the patch that appends: <code>if (turns.length &gt; 3)
        turns.shift()</code>. The card keeps three so the cap shows in a single press; a chat pane
        keeps fifty. Put the push and the trim in one function every writer calls, as
        <code>say</code> is here, and the cap belongs to the channel rather than to whichever caller
        remembered it.</p>

        <p><code>by: 'id'</code> is what makes the trim cheap on screen. Each message carries an id
        the app gives it, and a keyed <code>{'{#for}'}</code> moves the rows it keeps (32.29), so a
        send adds one row and drops one. Without <code>by</code> the rows are positional (32.13):
        trimming the front shifts every message up one place, and every row is rewritten.</p>

        <p>On the wire the trim is cheap too. A patch on a value a <code>GET</code> serves is sent
        to its followers as what it wrote rather than as the value (18.11), and a <code>shift</code>
        is one write.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/keep-the-last-few-values')}>Recent values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/repeat-markup-over-a-list')}>Lists</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`transform` refuses a message under a name you declared">
        <Example
            id="channel-transform"
            title={'`{ transform }` on a channel — a message refused by name'}
            summary="The composer holding a message longer than the conversation takes. Press Send, then cut it down and press again."
            files={{
                'lib/LimitedChat.abide': limitedChatSource,
            }}
        >
            <LimitedChat />
        </Example>

        <p><code>transform</code> is the same option every reactive value takes. It may rewrite the
        value or <strong>refuse</strong> it by returning a <code>Failed</code>, and the card's
        refuses a turn longer than the conversation takes. You declare the refusal once, and its
        name and data are the type on both sides — so <code>tooLong.is</code> narrows
        <code>error()</code> to it.</p>

        <p>A <code>patch</code> mutates the held array in place (5.7), so neither
        <code>schema</code> nor <code>transform</code> sees it. The card writes the whole array
        instead, <code>onboarding = [...onboarding, turn]</code>, and the transform checks the turn
        it ends on. A refused write stores nothing (4.9) and fills <code>error()</code> (4.10), so
        the last accepted turn stays on screen beside the message. <code>set</code> hands that
        refusal back too (4.18). The other spelling checks the turn before patching it in, and keeps
        the patch.</p>

        <p>It runs on <strong>every</strong> write, the app's own included — normalizing is not a
        question about who is asking. A message's <strong>shape</strong> is the sibling option,
        <code>schema</code>, and it is the channel's rather than the <code>GET</code>'s for the same
        reason (16.26).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Every write is gated by `identity`">
        <p>There is no ungated write to a channel. <code>identity</code> means what it means on
        every value, and a <code>set</code> of the value already held mints nothing (5.2). A turn
        said twice is two elements in the array, so a repeated message is still a change, and the
        channel holds both.</p>

        <p>A <code>set</code> on a subscriber's copy stands until the next value arrives and
        replaces it (9.4).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/slow-down-a-value-that-changes-too-fast')}>Throttle &amp;
        debounce</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Following a channel sees turns, not every message">
        <p><code>{'onboarding.watch((turns) => …)'}</code> follows the channel. The effect runs once
        at the end of a turn, however many writes landed in it, and a patch in place is one of them
        (12.10). A burst of three messages in one turn is one run, holding all three.</p>

        <p>So there is no exactly-once tail of a channel. Code that has to act on every message acts
        where it writes it — in <code>say</code>, beside the push.</p>

        <p><code>for await</code> is not a follow. Over a value it yields the settled value once and
        ends, as <code>await</code> does (2.5). Over a stream it yields each chunk of the run and ends
        at the close (2.6), and a capped window still delivers every chunk (5.10).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel is not a stream">
        <p>Both hold an array that grows, and who grows it separates them. A channel has no
        producer: anything appends to it, and every append is a whole message. A body that is or
        returns an async generator is a stream, and its array is the chunks its run has yielded
        (9.1).</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>the array grows by</th><th><code>busy()</code></th><th><code>await</code> resolves at</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>channel</td><td>a <code>patch</code> from anything that reaches it</td><td>never where it is written (3.9)</td><td>at once where it is written</td></tr>
                <tr><td>stream</td><td>a <strong>chunk</strong> from its own run</td><td>until the stream <strong>closes</strong> (3.7)</td><td>the close</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A channel does not finish, so being live is not being busy.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel lives as long as the value does">
        <p>Nothing discards a channel for having no subscribers. A keyed channel's entry lives until
        its <code>ttl</code> (11.45), and a write to it starts that life again, so a room nobody
        wrote to for an hour is let go. On a channel with no body there is no producer, and
        <code>ttl</code> is inert (6.2).</p>

        <p>A message that should expire on its own is the app's to drop: a <code>setTimeout</code>
        per message that patches it out. A restart drops every channel, and a conversation older
        than the process was always app data behind an ordinary rpc.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Channels are process-wide, not per caller">
        <p>The one thing to get right. A reactive value's default scope is one caller —
        request-local on a server (11.32) — and <strong>a channel every caller follows is the
        opposite</strong>. So it is built with <code>global: true</code>, which puts it in one
        process-wide cache that outlives every request (11.34). State every caller can reach is what
        a channel <em>is</em>, and <code>args</code> is how one caller's channel is told from
        another's.</p>

        <p>So on a server a channel is shared across requests by design. Anything caller-specific
        belongs in the <code>args</code>, exactly as it does for a <code>global</code> keyed
        value.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`reactive.share` is one caller; a channel is many">
        <p>They look alike — both give distant components one thing with no prop between them — and
        they are opposites underneath.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th><code>reactive.share</code></th><th>a channel</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>scope</td><td>one component and its descendants</td><td>process-wide, every caller</td></tr>
                <tr><td>before anyone writes</td><td>the value you created, <code>ready()</code></td><td>the array you opened it with, <code>ready()</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>lifetime</td><td>never evicted within its scope</td><td>the value's, or an entry's <code>ttl</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>typing</td><td>a key per name, each its own type</td><td>one element type across every channel</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The short version: <code>reactive.share</code> is <strong>one caller, many
        components</strong>. A channel is <strong>many callers, one subject</strong>. Reach for the
        first when the thing is yours and the path between readers is long; reach for a channel when
        the thing belongs to nobody.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/share-one-value-across-components')}>Sharing</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> — serving a
            channel to callers outside the process</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/keep-the-last-few-values')}>Recent values</a> — keeping the
            last few values, on a channel or anywhere</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/share-one-value-across-components')}>Sharing</a> — the
            one-caller counterpart</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
